<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Józef Piłsudski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Jozef Pilsudski</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Józef Piłsudski was a Polish revolutionary who led the Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legions</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and sat on the Provisional Council of State in 1917 before being interned by the Germans. In November 1918 he took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> as chief of state of the new Second Polish Republic, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in 1920 and ruled as strongman after his coup of 1926.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski.jpg" alt="Józef Piłsudski" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J%C3%B3zef_Pi%C5%82sudski_(-1930).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Józef Klemens Piłsudski was born on 5 December 1867 into a family of nobility at Zułow, near the village of Zułowo in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, then under Russian rule. He knew Lithuanian as well as Polish. The family was impoverished but cherished Polish patriotic traditions. Held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irkutsk">Irkutsk</a> while being taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, he was beaten with other political prisoners after a protest, and the cold of his first night in prison nearly killed him. In 1893 he joined the Polish Socialist Party, and in 1894 he became chief editor of its underground newspaper Robotnik. He remained a Polish nationalist and held that socialism and nationalism should be combined to win independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the early 1900s almost all parties in Russian Poland sought autonomy within the empire, while Piłsudski's party was prepared to fight for independence. In 1906, with the connivance of the Austrian authorities, he founded a military school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> for paramilitary units. On 3 August 1914 he formed the First Cadre Company in Kraków, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legions</a> fought alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In 1917 he withdrew support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>, and the Germans imprisoned him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>.</p><p>On 11 November 1918 the Regency Council appointed him Commander-in-Chief, and that day he proclaimed an independent Polish state. As Chief of State until 1922 he faced a country with nine legal systems, five currencies and 66 types of rail system. From 1919 to 1921 he commanded forces in six wars over the borders. In August 1920 his plan, a counteroffensive led by a Reserve Army of about 20,000 men, repelled the Soviet advance at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. On 14 December 1922 he handed his powers to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-narutowicz">Gabriel Narutowicz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After retiring in 1923, Piłsudski returned to power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/may-coup-poland">May Coup</a> of 1926. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> elected him president, but he refused the office and became Minister of Military Affairs, which he held until his death. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanation">Sanation</a> government sought to reduce the influence of parties and strengthen the army, and constitutional amendments of 2 August 1926 curtailed the Sejm. Its methods were at times authoritarian, and the imprisonment of opponents at Bereza Kartuska remains controversial. He died of liver cancer on 12 May 1935 at the Belweder Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. He is widely regarded as a founder of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and a father of the Second Polish Republic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Józef Piłsudski” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Polish troops took Vilnius from the Soviets on 21 April 1919, and in August 1919 <b>Józef Piłsudski</b> attempted to overthrow the government in Kaunas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Dictators also ruled Poland after <b>Józef Piłsudski</b>’s coup of 1926, Portugal under António de Oliveira Salazar, Spain under Francisco Franco from 1939, much of the Balkans and the Baltic states, and Brazil under Getúlio Vargas from 1937.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Dąbrowski's name was at times replaced by later leaders, from Józef Chłopicki to <b>Józef Piłsudski</b> and Władysław Sikorski.</blockquote><a href="/songs/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</a></li><li><blockquote>A Provisional Council of State, including <b>Jozef Pilsudski</b>, was set up in 1917, but real power remained with the German governor-general in Warsaw.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1916-1918">Kingdom of Poland (1916-1918)</a></li><li><blockquote>As the partitioning empires collapsed in November 1918, <b>Jozef Pilsudski</b> took power in Warsaw from the Regency Council of the Kingdom of Poland (1916-1918), and independence was proclaimed on 11 November.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zalavas, in what is now <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.97,25.98&amp;year=1911" aria-label="Zalavas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5120 284 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Józef Piłsudski"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5570 554h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5570 554h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.97,25.98&amp;year=1911">What was here in 1911? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
